Set
---
-  Sets are declared by using { }
-  Unordered collection of unique elements
-  In sets we don't have any index number

-Sets are unordered collection of elements
  s1 = {92, 5, 54, 22, 6, 4, 56, 1}
  
  print(s1)
  # {1, 4, 5, 6, 54, 22, 56, 92}

- Sets are always unique data, it never stores duplicates
  s1 = {92, 5, 54, 22, 6, 4, 56, 1, 22, 6}
  
  print(s1)
  # {1, 4, 5, 6, 54, 22, 56, 92}

Methods
--------
A = {1, 2, 3, 4}
B = {3, 4, 5, 6}

1. add()
--------
-  It is used to add specified element to your set
A.add(10)
print(A)                       # {1, 2, 3, 4, 10}

2. remove()
------------
- It is used to remove specified element from the set if available, 
  otherwise it will get KeyError.
A.remove(10)
print(A)                       # {1, 2, 3, 4}

3. pop()
--------
-  It will remove top most element in your set
s1.pop()

4. discard()
-------------
- It will remove a specified element from the set if available, 
  otherwise it will do nothing
s1.discard(52)

5. clear()
-----------
- It will remove all the elements in your set
s1.clear()

6. update()
------------
Add multiple elements
s = {1,2}
s.update([5, 9, 2])

A = {1, 2, 3, 4}
B = {3, 4, 5, 6}

7. Union
---------
print(A | B)                   # {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}
print(A.union(B))

8. Intersection
----------------
print(A & B)                   # {3, 4}
print(A.intersection(B))

9. Difference
--------------
print(A - B)                   # {1, 2}
print(A.difference(B))

10. Symmetric Difference
------------------------
print(A ^ B)                   # {1, 2, 5, 6}
print(A.symmetric_difference(B))

11. issubset()
--------------
Checks whether one set belongs to another
a = {1, 2}
b = {1, 2, 3, 4}
print(a.issubset(b))

12. isdisjoint()
----------------
Checks no common elements
a = {1, 2}
b = {3, 4}
print(a.isdisjoint(b))

13. copy() 
----------
– Copy set
D = B.copy()

 SET BUILT-IN FUNCTIONS
 -------------------------------

 len() – Number of elements
print(len(A))                  # 4

 type() – Data type
print(type(A))                 # <class 'set'>

 max() – Maximum element
print(max(A))                  # 4

 min() – Minimum element
print(min(A))                  # 1

 sum() – Sum of elements
print(sum(A))                  # 10

 sorted() – Sorted list
print(sorted(A))               # [1, 2, 3, 4]

 set() – Create empty set
C = set()

